# N, the induction incidence, projected to what a `categoryValueRef` carries. One row per
# (filing, operation, layer): this operation induces into this layer, and the flow node says so
# itself rather than being collected by a container.
#
# The direction is the whole difference
#   A `lane` collects its members and a flow node can be collected once. A `categoryValue` collects
#   nobody: the member declares which categories it is in, unbounded, so the cover may overlap and
#   no container has to be enlarged to say so. That is why this relation can be many-to-many where
#   diagrams/lane_membership.sqlc cannot.
#
# Exercised, not hypothetical
#   In this corpus an operation both draws from `labour` and induces into `capability`, so it
#   belongs to two layers, and a lane set can hold it in one. With no relation for the cover, an
#   emitter puts the operation in the draw lane and the induction is absent from the artifact
#   entirely.
#
# Incidence only
#   The wider relation carries three points, a unit, a typed absence and `decider`; none of it
#   composes here, for the reason diagrams/lane_members.sqlc gives.
#
# Defined, not read
#   A program reads these columns, so this statement defines its vocabulary in its own
#   WITH clause: a view keeps the rows and loses the column's origin, which is where
#   NOT NULL lives. assets/sqlc/views.sqlc says what a definition is.
#
-- pm:Operation/pm:Induction as incidence; BPMN 2.0 tFlowElement/categoryValueRef.
WITH
inductions AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(entries/inductions.sqlc)
)
SELECT i.filing, i.operation, i.layer
FROM (
    :compose(entries/inductions.sqlc)
) i
